struct S {
    S(int i) : I(i) {}  // Constructor call is part of the full-expression in initialization
    int& v() { return I; }  // Member function call could be a subexpression
    private:
    int I;
};

S s1(1);  // Full-expression: The entire statement, including constructor call and any implicit conversions
S s2 = 2;  // Full-expression: Includes copy/move initialization, with the compiler ensuring the expression is evaluated as a unit, potentially optimizing away temporaries if elidable

Implementation notes: In the syntax above, the full-expression for 'S s1(1);' encompasses the constructor invocation. The compiler must sequence the evaluation of arguments before the constructor call, and any side effects (e.g., in the constructor body) are part of this full-expression, ensuring proper memory layout for 'I' on the stack.

code_example:
#include <iostream>

struct S {
    S(int i) : I(i) { std::cout << "Constructor called with " << i << std::endl; }
    ~S() { std::cout << "Destructor called" << std::endl; }  // Demonstrates lifetime tied to full-expression
    private:
    int I;
};

int main() {
    S s1(1);  // Full-expression: Constructor is called, and object lifetime ends at ;
    S s2 = 2;  // Full-expression: Similar, but via copy/move initialization
    return 0;
}
